1
00:00:03,690 --> 00:00:06,420
Hey, would you like to hear some songs I've rewritten

2
00:00:06,420 --> 00:00:08,810
to get children interested in the hard sciences?

3
00:00:08,810 --> 00:00:10,260
- Sure. - Really?

4
00:00:10,260 --> 00:00:12,710
Yeah. Well, I like music, I like science,

5
00:00:12,710 --> 00:00:15,130
I like making fun of Sheldon. Hit it.

6
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:18,740
* There was a scientist who had a theory *

7
00:00:18,740 --> 00:00:20,680
* And James Clerk Maxwell was his name-o *

8
00:00:20,680 --> 00:00:22,520
* J-a-m-e-s *

9
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:24,330
* C-l-e-r-k *

10
00:00:24,330 --> 00:00:26,840
* Space M-A-X-W-E-L-L *

11
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:29,880
* And James Clerk Maxwell was his name-o *

12
00:00:30,290 --> 00:00:32,120
* There was a scientist who had a theory *

13
00:00:32,120 --> 00:00:34,110
* And James Clerk Maxwell was his name-o *

14
00:00:34,110 --> 00:00:35,700
* A-m-e-s *

15
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:37,580
Uh, okay, okay. Uh, we-we get it.

16
00:00:37,580 --> 00:00:40,190
Perhaps you'd prefer this one.

17
00:00:40,190 --> 00:00:44,900
* The itsy bitsy spider is not an insect at all *

18
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:47,050
* Because it has eight legs *

19
00:00:47,050 --> 00:00:50,120
* And two body parts *

20
00:00:50,770 --> 00:00:52,030
That's pretty cool, Sheldon.

21
00:00:52,030 --> 00:00:53,180
Thank you.

22
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:55,350
Do either of you know Beyonce?

23
00:00:55,350 --> 00:00:58,500
I'd love her to get behind it.

24
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:02,390
You guys know the new Discovery class missions

25
00:01:02,390 --> 00:01:04,190
- that NASA's been working on? - Yeah.

26
00:01:04,190 --> 00:01:06,220
Well, they're looking to include a message from Earth

27
00:01:06,220 --> 00:01:08,960
in case one of them is encountered by alien life.

28
00:01:09,540 --> 00:01:11,520
When I encountered alien life, I-I discovered

29
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:15,230
that the key thing was not to sit in its spot.

30
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:18,880
All right, you can't breathe our air without an inhaler,

31
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:20,260
he's allergic to Earth nuts,

32
00:01:20,260 --> 00:01:22,460
but I'm the alien.

33
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:25,760
Anyway, I'm among a handful of scientists

34
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:28,030
that have been asked to submit a design proposal

35
00:01:28,030 --> 00:01:30,080
for the message and its delivery system.

36
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:31,600
- Excellent. - Congratulations.

37
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:34,340
And I was wondering if any of you guys

38
00:01:34,340 --> 00:01:35,860
would like to help me do it.

39
00:01:35,860 --> 00:01:38,140
Are you kidding? Yes. What did you have in mind?

40
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:40,130
I'll tell you exactly what you should do:

41
00:01:40,130 --> 00:01:43,360
avoid the presumption of the Terran sensory input paradigm.

42
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:44,120
Yeah, absolutely.

43
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:46,320
You need a device capable of delivering information

44
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:48,690
across a wide range of perceptual modalities.

45
00:01:48,690 --> 00:01:51,350
Any intelligent organism would at the very least need

46
00:01:51,350 --> 00:01:53,830
the ability to locate the position of objects in space.

47
00:01:53,830 --> 00:01:57,320
So the ideal interstellar lingua franca would be haptic.

48
00:01:57,320 --> 00:01:59,400
Ooh, how about a 3-D tactile communicator rigged

49
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:00,710
for cross-sensory transposition?

50
00:02:00,710 --> 00:02:02,360
Exactly what I expected.

51
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:03,870
Two people forcing their ideas on me

52
00:02:03,870 --> 00:02:05,770
and only one gentleman who could be bothered

53
00:02:05,770 --> 00:02:07,520
to ask me what my thoughts were.

54
00:02:07,520 --> 00:02:09,380
You two are out. Congratulations, Leonard,

55
00:02:09,380 --> 00:02:11,390
you're on the team.

56
00:02:11,780 --> 00:02:14,390
My mommy raised a gentleman.

57
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:42,500
Hey. Thanks for coming by.

58
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:43,880
Yeah. I'm excited to help.

59
00:02:43,880 --> 00:02:45,930
I would have included the others, but...

60
00:02:45,930 --> 00:02:47,660
you know exactly what would've happened.

61
00:02:47,660 --> 00:02:50,380
They would've taken over the project and bossed us around.

62
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:51,340
I get it.

63
00:02:51,340 --> 00:02:52,580
Uh, just this morning,

64
00:02:52,580 --> 00:02:54,780
Sheldon wouldn't let me put almond milk on my Grape-Nuts

65
00:02:54,780 --> 00:02:58,310
because he said it was a theoretical nut conflict.

66
00:02:59,110 --> 00:03:00,820
You should have told him to mind his own business.

67
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:03,370
Yeah. That's better than what I did say, which was,

68
00:03:03,370 --> 00:03:06,160
"Fine, I'll eat them with club soda."

69
00:03:06,580 --> 00:03:09,010
What makes them think they're always in charge of everything?

70
00:03:09,010 --> 00:03:10,680
Mmm, they're alpha males.

71
00:03:11,160 --> 00:03:12,180
What does that make us?

72
00:03:12,180 --> 00:03:14,310
We could be betas. They're second in charge.

73
00:03:14,310 --> 00:03:15,330
Okay, that sounds good.

74
00:03:15,330 --> 00:03:16,750
Or we could be omegas.

75
00:03:16,750 --> 00:03:19,080
They get pushed around by the alphas and the betas.

76
00:03:19,080 --> 00:03:21,000
Okay, that sounds like us.

77
00:03:22,220 --> 00:03:24,150
Whatever. There's no alphas here,

78
00:03:24,150 --> 00:03:25,520
and this is your project.

79
00:03:25,520 --> 00:03:28,130
You're in charge. How do you want to start?

80
00:03:28,890 --> 00:03:31,260
I don't know. How do you want to start?

81
00:03:32,950 --> 00:03:34,880
I don't know.

82
00:03:37,480 --> 00:03:39,750
Should we call Sheldon and Howard?

83
00:03:39,750 --> 00:03:41,800
No. We can do this by ourselves.

84
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:43,370
Okay. Great. How do you want to start?

85
00:03:43,370 --> 00:03:45,400
I don't know. How do you want to start?

86
00:03:50,920 --> 00:03:51,720
Sheldon.

87
00:03:51,970 --> 00:03:53,180
Sheldon.

88
00:03:53,180 --> 00:03:55,200
Sheldon.

89
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:04,100
I bet that started off as a joke,

90
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:06,470
but by the third one you realized

91
00:04:06,470 --> 00:04:10,290
there was something strangely enjoyable about it.

92
00:04:11,080 --> 00:04:13,950
Yeah, I kind of want to do it again.

93
00:04:13,950 --> 00:04:17,560
I don't recommend it. You'll be doing it the rest of your life.

94
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:21,220
Anyway, if you're looking for Leonard, he's with Koothrappali.

95
00:04:21,220 --> 00:04:22,700
Uh, no, I actually came to talk to you.

96
00:04:23,070 --> 00:04:25,550
How nice.

97
00:04:26,650 --> 00:04:28,430
Here are some topics that interest me:

98
00:04:28,430 --> 00:04:31,140
quantum mechanics, trains, flags...

99
00:04:31,140 --> 00:04:33,120
No, no. It's about my acting career.

100
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:34,040
Oh, sorry.

101
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:36,040
That's not on the list.

102
00:04:36,040 --> 00:04:37,050
Oh, wait. No.

103
00:04:37,050 --> 00:04:39,200
How about we split the difference

104
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:42,400
and discuss why Austria was an archduchy

105
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:44,760
and not just a regular duchy.

106
00:04:45,420 --> 00:04:46,670
Okay, look, here's the thing.

107
00:04:46,670 --> 00:04:48,880
I like pharmaceutical sales--it's going great--

108
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:50,270
but I have an audition for a movie,

109
00:04:50,270 --> 00:04:52,470
and if I get it, it could screw everything up.

110
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:55,610
I know exactly what you should do.

111
00:04:55,610 --> 00:04:58,260
Unfortunately, I cannot tell you.

112
00:04:59,550 --> 00:05:00,620
Well, why?

113
00:05:00,620 --> 00:05:03,390
I'm attempting to turn over a new leaf.

114
00:05:03,390 --> 00:05:05,340
Earlier today, it was pointed out to me

115
00:05:05,340 --> 00:05:08,690
that I tend to force my ideas on people.

116
00:05:09,100 --> 00:05:10,990
You're really not gonna tell me?

117
00:05:10,990 --> 00:05:14,310
No, that train has left the station.

118
00:05:14,310 --> 00:05:16,960
Now, we can play this one of two ways.

119
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:19,680
You can say, "Trains, tell me more,"

120
00:05:19,680 --> 00:05:20,880
or...

121
00:05:20,880 --> 00:05:22,570
you can just look at me like that

122
00:05:22,570 --> 00:05:23,880
and I'll start.

123
00:05:28,830 --> 00:05:30,320
What's going on in here?

124
00:05:30,320 --> 00:05:33,600
I am making molecular cocktails.

125
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:37,260
This sphere is actually a cosmopolitan.

126
00:05:38,140 --> 00:05:39,170
How do you drink it?

127
00:05:39,170 --> 00:05:42,330
Oh, just put it in your mouth and pop it like a zit.

128
00:05:43,770 --> 00:05:45,650
I think I'll have a beer.

129
00:05:46,540 --> 00:05:47,920
First take a picture with me.

130
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:48,580
Why?

131
00:05:48,580 --> 00:05:50,940
Well, Raj and I always talked about learning

132
00:05:50,940 --> 00:05:53,370
how to make cocktails like this together,

133
00:05:53,370 --> 00:05:57,190
so I taught myself and I'm putting this on Instagram

134
00:05:57,190 --> 00:05:59,270
so he can see it and feel like a turd.

135
00:05:59,270 --> 00:06:00,370
Say cheese.

136
00:06:01,820 --> 00:06:04,130
Is this about the space probe he's working on without you?

137
00:06:04,130 --> 00:06:06,290
You betcha. The very one.

138
00:06:07,910 --> 00:06:09,000
Howard, you're grown men.

139
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,100
You guys don't have to do everything together.

140
00:06:11,100 --> 00:06:15,130
I know. That's why I'm spending tonight with you.

141
00:06:15,770 --> 00:06:17,330
Trying to hurt Raj's feelings.

142
00:06:17,330 --> 00:06:20,160
With my honeybunch.

143
00:06:20,710 --> 00:06:22,010
You're being childish.

144
00:06:22,010 --> 00:06:23,840
No. He is.

145
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:26,790
So I have a dominant personality.

146
00:06:26,790 --> 00:06:28,540
We all know that.

147
00:06:28,930 --> 00:06:31,220
I'm sorry. What do we know?

148
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:33,920
In social groupings,

149
00:06:33,920 --> 00:06:37,830
I just naturally wind up in charge.

150
00:06:37,830 --> 00:06:38,970
Don't take this the wrong way,

151
00:06:38,970 --> 00:06:42,280
but how many of these little booze balls have you had?

152
00:06:43,720 --> 00:06:45,370
You don't think I'm a leader?

153
00:06:45,370 --> 00:06:47,260
I was kidding. Of course you are.

154
00:06:47,260 --> 00:06:49,880
Right. I couldn't change if I wanted to.

155
00:06:49,880 --> 00:06:53,130
You better not change, because I love who you are.

156
00:06:53,130 --> 00:06:55,470
Now, will you need help cleaning all this up when you're done

157
00:06:55,470 --> 00:06:57,450
or can you do it all by yourself?

158
00:06:57,450 --> 00:06:58,850
All by myself.

159
00:06:58,850 --> 00:07:00,920
There's my big boss man.

160
00:07:04,720 --> 00:07:08,130
So, often on the front of the locomotive

161
00:07:08,130 --> 00:07:10,860
is a large iron wedge

162
00:07:10,860 --> 00:07:13,190
for clearing objects off the tracks.

163
00:07:13,190 --> 00:07:16,080
Now, while commonly known as a cowcatcher,

164
00:07:16,080 --> 00:07:20,790
I prefer the more accurate "Cow exploder."

165
00:07:22,190 --> 00:07:24,440
Okay. Sheldon, let me ask you a question.

166
00:07:24,440 --> 00:07:26,370
If I was at a train station

167
00:07:26,370 --> 00:07:29,190
and one train could take me to my current job

168
00:07:29,190 --> 00:07:31,990
and the other train could take me to an audition for a movie,

169
00:07:31,990 --> 00:07:34,550
which train should I get on?

170
00:07:37,540 --> 00:07:41,850
Are you using trains to trick me into giving you advice?

171
00:07:42,260 --> 00:07:44,260
No.

172
00:07:44,260 --> 00:07:46,350
All right, then.

173
00:07:47,510 --> 00:07:49,650
You should take a third train

174
00:07:49,650 --> 00:07:51,740
where you audition for the movie

175
00:07:51,740 --> 00:07:53,850
but hold off on making a career decision

176
00:07:53,850 --> 00:07:55,980
until you have more information.

177
00:07:56,780 --> 00:07:57,410
You're right.

178
00:07:57,410 --> 00:08:00,320
I'm worried about something that hasn't even happened yet.

179
00:08:00,910 --> 00:08:02,650
You are a wise man.

180
00:08:03,550 --> 00:08:06,300
Well, Penny, who's smarter: the wise man

181
00:08:06,300 --> 00:08:10,150
or the person who comes to him for advice?

182
00:08:11,940 --> 00:08:12,840
Oh, I guess you're right.

183
00:08:12,840 --> 00:08:15,630
Maybe it is the person who asks.

184
00:08:15,630 --> 00:08:18,280
No, it's the wise man.

185
00:08:21,400 --> 00:08:24,460
That's why he's called the wise man.

186
00:08:24,940 --> 00:08:26,570
You know how I know that?

187
00:08:26,570 --> 00:08:28,420
I'm the wise man.

188
00:08:30,080 --> 00:08:32,110
I'm sorry. What was I thinking?

189
00:08:32,510 --> 00:08:34,030
Uh, just out of curiosity,

190
00:08:34,030 --> 00:08:36,620
why didn't you ask Leonard for advice about this?

191
00:08:36,620 --> 00:08:38,180
'Cause I already know what he'll say.

192
00:08:38,180 --> 00:08:40,920
"Wah, wah, wah, you shouldn't do it."

193
00:08:40,920 --> 00:08:44,100
It's just like he's here!

194
00:08:46,280 --> 00:08:49,300
Okay, so we know that previous attempts to send a message into space,

195
00:08:49,300 --> 00:08:51,040
like the Golden Record on Voyager,

196
00:08:51,040 --> 00:08:52,180
were heavily criticized.

197
00:08:52,180 --> 00:08:54,200
Well, aliens could only play the Golden Record

198
00:08:54,200 --> 00:08:56,350
if they figured out how to build a record player.

199
00:08:56,350 --> 00:08:59,640
Although, to be fair, I watched E.T. build a telephone

200
00:08:59,640 --> 00:09:02,640
out of a Speak & Spell and an umbrella.

201
00:09:02,940 --> 00:09:05,150
Dude was like a little brown MacGyver.

202
00:09:05,880 --> 00:09:08,170
That would be more helpful if E.T. were real.

203
00:09:08,170 --> 00:09:12,720
Well, my feelings were real when he was about to die.

204
00:09:13,940 --> 00:09:17,350
My God, when he's on the table and they use the paddles on him.

205
00:09:17,350 --> 00:09:18,650
And he's all white.

206
00:09:18,650 --> 00:09:20,770
They zip him up in that bag.

207
00:09:20,770 --> 00:09:23,000
And Gertie can't stop crying.

208
00:09:23,530 --> 00:09:25,590
The flower dies.

209
00:09:26,390 --> 00:09:28,960
- Okay, let's talk about something else.- All right.

210
00:09:28,960 --> 00:09:32,390
So it-it sounds like we need a way to communicate that's simple.

211
00:09:32,390 --> 00:09:34,370
And doesn't require outside machinery

212
00:09:34,370 --> 00:09:35,510
to be built to access it.

213
00:09:35,510 --> 00:09:37,560
It's also a problem because we don't even know

214
00:09:37,560 --> 00:09:39,580
if the aliens who find this can see.

215
00:09:39,580 --> 00:09:40,640
I mean, they might communicate

216
00:09:40,640 --> 00:09:42,550
in a totally different way than us.

217
00:09:42,550 --> 00:09:44,320
Like when my dog is mad at me,

218
00:09:44,320 --> 00:09:47,030
she tells me by peeing in my slippers.

219
00:09:48,090 --> 00:09:50,600
That's actually a valid example.

220
00:09:50,600 --> 00:09:53,410
Animals do deliver messages through scent.

221
00:09:53,410 --> 00:09:55,320
Bees talk to each other by dancing.

222
00:09:55,320 --> 00:09:56,490
Whales have their songs.

223
00:09:56,490 --> 00:09:58,950
Penny has about 20 different ways of rolling her eyes

224
00:09:58,950 --> 00:10:00,890
that each mean something different.

225
00:10:01,490 --> 00:10:03,570
Okay, so, what it sounds like is,

226
00:10:03,570 --> 00:10:04,860
what we want is a device

227
00:10:04,860 --> 00:10:06,830
that can deliver a message through not only sight,

228
00:10:06,830 --> 00:10:08,380
but other senses, as well.

229
00:10:08,380 --> 00:10:11,940
The most basic sense that any space-faring civilization

230
00:10:11,940 --> 00:10:14,380
would have to possess is touch.

231
00:10:15,680 --> 00:10:18,050
We could make a video-playing device

232
00:10:18,050 --> 00:10:20,560
that simultaneously translates the information

233
00:10:20,560 --> 00:10:21,850
into a tactile medium.

234
00:10:21,850 --> 00:10:24,530
All we have to do is rig up a 3-D communication system!

235
00:10:24,530 --> 00:10:25,820
We-we can totally do that!

236
00:10:25,820 --> 00:10:26,500
I know!

237
00:10:26,500 --> 00:10:27,150
This is great!

238
00:10:27,150 --> 00:10:28,250
- Yeah, you know what else it is? - What?

239
00:10:28,250 --> 00:10:30,920
Exactly what Sheldon and Howard said.

240
00:10:33,060 --> 00:10:35,930
Well, thank you for peeing in my slippers.

241
00:10:40,390 --> 00:10:42,580
* It's the eye of the tiger *

242
00:10:42,580 --> 00:10:44,300
* It's the ear of the bat *

243
00:10:44,300 --> 00:10:46,640
* It's the whiskers of the catfish *

244
00:10:46,640 --> 00:10:49,420
* And the walrus... *

245
00:10:50,480 --> 00:10:54,730
Hang on. Not that your song isn't terrible-- it is...

246
00:10:55,750 --> 00:10:59,060
...but how do you mention bats and leave out sonar?

247
00:10:59,060 --> 00:11:00,760
You didn't let me finish.

248
00:11:00,760 --> 00:11:04,980
* And also regarding the bat *

249
00:11:04,980 --> 00:11:07,660
* It has sonar. *

250
00:11:10,030 --> 00:11:11,040
Hello.

251
00:11:12,170 --> 00:11:13,100
Look,

252
00:11:13,920 --> 00:11:17,070
I know you guys are upset, but we've talked about it,

253
00:11:17,070 --> 00:11:18,590
and we think it'd be beneficial

254
00:11:18,590 --> 00:11:20,590
for you to be part of the project.

255
00:11:21,090 --> 00:11:24,970
Well, well, well, did you hear that, Sheldon?

256
00:11:24,970 --> 00:11:27,030
I'm sorry. I was trying to think of what rhymes

257
00:11:27,030 --> 00:11:29,100
with "Nose of the aardvark."

258
00:11:30,790 --> 00:11:32,900
We want you back on the project with us.

259
00:11:32,900 --> 00:11:38,790
Well, well, well, did you hear that, Howard?

260
00:11:38,790 --> 00:11:39,490
Look, we admit it.

261
00:11:39,490 --> 00:11:42,190
The idea that you guys came up with was really good,

262
00:11:42,190 --> 00:11:44,350
and I'd love your help.

263
00:11:44,350 --> 00:11:46,930
I suppose it couldn't have been easy for you to say that.

264
00:11:46,930 --> 00:11:51,180
It wasn't, so... are you in?

265
00:11:51,760 --> 00:11:52,790
- Certainly. - Sure.

266
00:11:52,790 --> 00:11:53,690
Great.

267
00:11:53,690 --> 00:11:55,290
Now that we're all on the same page,

268
00:11:55,290 --> 00:11:57,120
let's get together tonight and work on it.

269
00:11:57,120 --> 00:11:58,330
"Get together tonight"?

270
00:11:58,330 --> 00:12:00,420
Leonard, stop trying to control everything,

271
00:12:00,420 --> 00:12:02,900
and give poor Raj a chance to come up with what we should do.

272
00:12:02,900 --> 00:12:03,960
Go ahead, Raj.

273
00:12:03,960 --> 00:12:05,650
Okay, I think we should do it right now.

274
00:12:05,650 --> 00:12:08,100
- Tonight works better for me. - Okay.

275
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,690
Okay, it's just an audition.

276
00:12:17,690 --> 00:12:19,270
Why am I nervous?

277
00:12:19,270 --> 00:12:20,650
Maybe it's a good thing.

278
00:12:20,650 --> 00:12:22,380
Just means I want it.

279
00:12:22,700 --> 00:12:24,580
And I can have it.

280
00:12:24,580 --> 00:12:25,680
This feels right.

281
00:12:25,680 --> 00:12:28,450
Why did I ever give this up?

282
00:12:32,800 --> 00:12:35,260
I'm starting to remember.

283
00:12:37,490 --> 00:12:39,700
So, I'd like to try a technique

284
00:12:39,700 --> 00:12:42,160
where no one gets steamrolled.

285
00:12:42,400 --> 00:12:43,330
When you talk,

286
00:12:43,330 --> 00:12:45,090
instead of bringing up a new idea,

287
00:12:45,090 --> 00:12:48,410
respect what was just said by building on it.

288
00:12:48,410 --> 00:12:50,850
Uh, building on that...

289
00:12:50,850 --> 00:12:53,210
...we should order dinner.

290
00:12:53,550 --> 00:12:55,210
How is that building on what he just said?

291
00:12:55,210 --> 00:12:59,310
Building on what Sheldon said, I could go for Chinese.

292
00:12:59,310 --> 00:13:00,980
Hang on. Building on what Leonard said,

293
00:13:00,980 --> 00:13:02,440
no one built on what I said.

294
00:13:02,440 --> 00:13:04,160
Building on "building on that,"

295
00:13:04,160 --> 00:13:07,100
there's a new build-your-own pizza place on Colorado.

296
00:13:07,690 --> 00:13:09,150
Building on that, I'd like to remind you,

297
00:13:09,150 --> 00:13:10,310
I'm lactose intolerant.

298
00:13:10,310 --> 00:13:11,840
I saw the menu. They have soy cheese.

299
00:13:12,310 --> 00:13:14,510
You didn't say "building on." You're out!

300
00:13:14,510 --> 00:13:15,720
It's not Simon Says.

301
00:13:15,720 --> 00:13:16,830
Yeah, you're missing the point, Sheldon.

302
00:13:16,830 --> 00:13:17,760
You're out, and you're out.

303
00:13:17,760 --> 00:13:19,910
I win! Who wants pizza?

304
00:13:24,060 --> 00:13:25,270
Penny?

305
00:13:26,900 --> 00:13:28,550
I haven't seen you auditioning in a while.

306
00:13:28,550 --> 00:13:31,300
Yeah, uh, I got a job as a pharmaceutical sales rep.

307
00:13:31,300 --> 00:13:32,430
You quit acting?

308
00:13:32,430 --> 00:13:33,440
Well, kind of.

309
00:13:33,440 --> 00:13:34,470
But now I get to act like

310
00:13:34,470 --> 00:13:38,070
inflamed heart is only a "Mild side effect."

311
00:13:39,140 --> 00:13:41,030
I heard you can make good money doing that.

312
00:13:41,030 --> 00:13:44,440
Yeah, it's going okay, but I do miss this sometimes.

313
00:13:44,440 --> 00:13:45,570
Really?

314
00:13:45,570 --> 00:13:48,780
'Cause I got to tell you, I am so sick of the humiliation

315
00:13:48,780 --> 00:13:50,810
and being treated like a piece of meat.

316
00:13:50,810 --> 00:13:51,750
Chelsea?

317
00:13:52,130 --> 00:13:53,740
Wish me luck.

318
00:13:56,090 --> 00:13:57,760
They're gonna love you.

319
00:13:58,390 --> 00:14:01,840
I heard she's pushing 40 and everything's fake.

320
00:14:02,580 --> 00:14:05,840
Yeah. I started that rumor.

321
00:14:07,390 --> 00:14:10,620
Okay, since we agree on the delivery system for the message,

322
00:14:10,620 --> 00:14:13,180
maybe we should talk about what the message could be.

323
00:14:13,180 --> 00:14:15,800
Well, I think we should show what earthlings look like.

324
00:14:15,800 --> 00:14:17,610
The plaque they sent up on the Pioneer probe

325
00:14:17,610 --> 00:14:19,650
had a drawing of a naked man and woman on it.

326
00:14:19,650 --> 00:14:21,380
Yeah, I never cared for that.

327
00:14:21,380 --> 00:14:23,900
It's advertising to predator races

328
00:14:23,900 --> 00:14:27,830
just how soft and squishy we are.

329
00:14:27,830 --> 00:14:29,210
Squeeze yourself!

330
00:14:29,210 --> 00:14:31,040
Oh, don't be offended.

331
00:14:31,040 --> 00:14:32,350
You know, of the four of us,

332
00:14:32,350 --> 00:14:35,530
you have the most veal-like consistency.

333
00:14:36,110 --> 00:14:38,710
Well, maybe there's a way to appear nonaggressive,

334
00:14:38,710 --> 00:14:41,330
but also be able to protect ourselves if necessary.

335
00:14:41,330 --> 00:14:43,770
Like smiling and waving with one hand,

336
00:14:43,770 --> 00:14:47,280
but the other hand holding the severed head of a tiger.

337
00:14:47,750 --> 00:14:49,560
You want to send a passive-aggressive message

338
00:14:49,560 --> 00:14:50,630
out into the universe?

339
00:14:50,630 --> 00:14:51,660
That's ridiculous.

340
00:14:51,660 --> 00:14:53,320
Oh, you mean passive-aggressive

341
00:14:53,320 --> 00:14:54,790
like posting pictures of you and your wife

342
00:14:54,790 --> 00:14:58,740
eating cosmopolitan balls that you know belong in my mouth?

343
00:15:00,540 --> 00:15:02,980
Perhaps Howard meant passive-aggressive

344
00:15:02,980 --> 00:15:05,530
like asking our group to help on your project,

345
00:15:05,530 --> 00:15:07,860
and then only choosing Leonard.

346
00:15:07,860 --> 00:15:10,350
Look, I'm sorry I hurt your feelings,

347
00:15:10,350 --> 00:15:12,580
but you do have strong personalities

348
00:15:12,580 --> 00:15:14,420
and always end up taking over.

349
00:15:14,420 --> 00:15:15,950
It's not always.

350
00:15:15,950 --> 00:15:17,620
What about when we went to Comic-Con

351
00:15:17,620 --> 00:15:19,240
and dressed like Jabba the Hutt?

352
00:15:19,240 --> 00:15:20,670
You got to be Jabba's head,

353
00:15:20,670 --> 00:15:23,890
and I got to be his fat slug butt.

354
00:15:25,240 --> 00:15:27,130
We looked great.

355
00:15:28,260 --> 00:15:30,500
You let a guy sit on me.

356
00:15:30,500 --> 00:15:32,430
He was dressed as Princess Leia.

357
00:15:32,430 --> 00:15:34,000
It made a nice picture.

358
00:15:36,100 --> 00:15:39,340
I'm just saying, sometimes Raj and I feel pushed aside.

359
00:15:39,340 --> 00:15:41,690
Yeah. Like when you took Sheldon to Texas

360
00:15:41,690 --> 00:15:43,440
and showed him all around NASA--

361
00:15:43,440 --> 00:15:45,480
you didn't even think to ask me and Leonard.

362
00:15:46,170 --> 00:15:47,320
You know what? You're right.

363
00:15:47,960 --> 00:15:49,280
I should have asked you.

364
00:15:49,280 --> 00:15:51,480
Yeah, and if he does ask you, go.

365
00:15:51,480 --> 00:15:53,220
It's amazing.

366
00:15:54,640 --> 00:15:57,050
Okay, and if we're talking about being left out of things,

367
00:15:57,050 --> 00:15:59,310
you guys went to Skywalker Ranch without us.

368
00:15:59,310 --> 00:16:00,770
Oh, I recommend that, too.

369
00:16:00,770 --> 00:16:03,210
That was a magical experience!

370
00:16:03,780 --> 00:16:04,390
Wait a minute.

371
00:16:04,390 --> 00:16:06,860
Sheldon spent a whole day with James Earl Jones

372
00:16:06,860 --> 00:16:08,300
and never told any of us.

373
00:16:08,300 --> 00:16:09,710
I sure did.

374
00:16:09,710 --> 00:16:11,450
Oh, my goodness!

375
00:16:11,450 --> 00:16:14,650
Well, from Jabba's head to ice cream with Darth Vader,

376
00:16:14,650 --> 00:16:16,970
I'm having a heck of a ride.

377
00:16:16,970 --> 00:16:17,900
Yeah, look,

378
00:16:17,900 --> 00:16:21,160
clearly, good things happen when I'm in charge.

379
00:16:21,160 --> 00:16:23,230
Now, why don't you boys step aside,

380
00:16:23,230 --> 00:16:25,090
let me knock this project out?

381
00:16:25,400 --> 00:16:26,660
Sheldon, you're not in charge.

382
00:16:26,660 --> 00:16:27,970
Raj is in charge.

383
00:16:27,970 --> 00:16:30,520
Leonard, who's really in charge?

384
00:16:30,520 --> 00:16:35,400
The person in charge, or those who put him in charge?

385
00:16:35,400 --> 00:16:36,660
He's right.

386
00:16:36,660 --> 00:16:38,850
If you think about it, we're all in charge.

387
00:16:38,850 --> 00:16:40,340
No, the guy in charge is in...

388
00:16:40,340 --> 00:16:42,980
Why is everyone so bad at these?!

389
00:16:44,650 --> 00:16:46,130
I mean, the whole experience reminded me

390
00:16:46,130 --> 00:16:47,920
about how much I hated about that world.

391
00:16:47,920 --> 00:16:51,530
You-you know, the anxiety, the depression, the negativity.

392
00:16:51,530 --> 00:16:52,970
I don't want to feel those things.

393
00:16:52,970 --> 00:16:56,690
I want to sell drugs to people who feel those things.

394
00:16:57,740 --> 00:16:59,540
I can't believe you got up and walked out

395
00:16:59,540 --> 00:17:01,630
of an audition for a big movie.

396
00:17:01,630 --> 00:17:02,830
I did.

397
00:17:02,830 --> 00:17:04,720
I-I mean, I walked in, read for the part,

398
00:17:04,720 --> 00:17:07,620
then stunk up the place, but then I walked right out.

399
00:17:08,930 --> 00:17:11,270
Well, I'm glad you have a new appreciation for your job.

400
00:17:11,340 --> 00:17:12,510
I do, and you know, I don't think

401
00:17:12,510 --> 00:17:15,530
I've ever thanked you properly for helping me get it.

402
00:17:15,530 --> 00:17:17,070
Properly, at all.

403
00:17:17,070 --> 00:17:19,820
It's just words I've never heard.

404
00:17:20,280 --> 00:17:21,010
Well, thank you.

405
00:17:21,010 --> 00:17:23,330
You're a good friend, and you changed my life.

406
00:17:23,330 --> 00:17:24,880
You're welcome.

407
00:17:24,880 --> 00:17:26,550
Hey, now that you're making some real money,

408
00:17:26,550 --> 00:17:29,480
maybe you can take your friend out for a nice thank-you dinner.

409
00:17:30,170 --> 00:17:31,230
Sure.

410
00:17:31,230 --> 00:17:33,090
And you probably have to invite your other friend

411
00:17:33,090 --> 00:17:34,600
'cause she overheard you talking about it,

412
00:17:34,600 --> 00:17:37,160
and it would be awkward to exclude her.

413
00:17:37,750 --> 00:17:38,790
Okay.

414
00:17:38,790 --> 00:17:40,330
How about now?

415
00:17:40,880 --> 00:17:42,450
All right.

416
00:17:44,800 --> 00:17:45,670
Don't forget your wallet.

417
00:17:45,670 --> 00:17:46,660
I...

418
00:17:56,660 --> 00:17:58,730
Greetings from planet Earth.

419
00:18:00,560 --> 00:18:02,850
Just turn left at Alpha Centauri.

420
00:18:02,850 --> 00:18:04,760
You can't miss it.

